<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/intermediate/telescopes-and-the-electromagnetic-spectrum">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Astronomy is a science done at a distance: no experiment, no sample return, no second look
    from a different angle — only the light (and gravity, and particles) that arrives. The
    telescope is how faint, distant things become measurable, and its operating rules are
    disarmingly simple. First rule: <strong>aperture beats everything</strong> — light-gathering
    grows with the <em>area</em> of the objective (mirror or lens), so doubling the diameter
    quadruples the photons. The 10-metre Keck telescopes collect on the order of a million times
    more light than your dark-adapted eye. Second rule: visible light is a sliver of the story;
    the universe broadcasts across the whole <strong>electromagnetic spectrum</strong>, and each
    band needs its own kind of eye — and some eyes must leave the planet entirely.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="why size wins — double the mirror, four times the photons; ten times, a hundred" widget="plotter" config={scopeCfg} />

  <h2>Objectives: lenses versus mirrors</h2>
  <p>
    A telescope's business end — the <strong>objective</strong> — collects light and brings it to
    a focus. Refractors use a lens: Galileo's 1609 instrument and every spyglass since. Lenses
    have two handicaps at scale: light of different colours bends by different amounts
    (<strong>chromatic aberration</strong>, the colour fringing around bright objects), and a
    large lens can only be supported at its rim, so it sags under its own weight. Reflectors use
    a curved <strong>mirror</strong>: all wavelengths focus identically (no chromatic error), and
    a mirror can be supported from behind across its whole back, which is why every large modern
    telescope — Keck's 10 m, the Vera Rubin Observatory's 8.4 m, the 39-metre Extreme Large
    Telescope under construction in Chile — is a reflector. In both designs, magnification is
    set by the <strong>eyepiece</strong> (focal length of objective ÷ focal length of eyepiece),
    which is why eyepieces are swappable and aperture — decided at purchase, unchangeable — is
    the number that really matters.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example in a hobbyist's vocabulary. A small reflector with a 1,000 mm focal length
    fitted with a 25 mm eyepiece magnifies 1,000 ÷ 25 = <strong>40×</strong>; swap in a 10 mm
    eyepiece and the same tube gives 100×. Swap in a 4 mm and you get 250× — but through a
    10 cm aperture that exceeds the useful limit (a rule of thumb: no more than about 2× the
    aperture in millimetres, so 200× for this scope), and the image grows dimmer and mushier
    rather than more detailed. Meanwhile the 40× view through the same scope gathers roughly
    (100/7)² ≈ 200 times the light of your naked eye — enough to show the rings of Saturn,
    the bands of Jupiter, and thousands of stars invisible without it. That is the practical
    hierarchy: aperture first, magnification second, and only as much of it as the aperture
    can support.
  </p>

  <h2>Resolution: the other reason size wins</h2>
  <p>
    Light is a wave, and waves through an aperture spread slightly — <strong>diffraction</strong>.
    The result is that even a perfect telescope cannot focus a star to a point: it forms a small
    disc surrounded by rings, and the disc's angular size shrinks as aperture grows. Two stars
    closer together than that limit blur into one. The diffraction limit is approximately
    θ ≈ 1.22 × λ / D (in radians), so bigger D means sharper images — the second dividend of
    aperture. Run the numbers: a 10 cm telescope at visible wavelengths (λ ≈ 550 nm) resolves
    about 1.4 arcseconds; the 2.4-metre Hubble resolves about 0.06 arcseconds, over twenty times
    finer — enough to separate individual stars in neighbouring galaxies. Magnification without
    aperture is <strong>empty magnification</strong>: enlarging a blurred image gives a bigger
    blur. Every serious buyer's advice follows from this — choose aperture first.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the diffraction limit — resolution improves as aperture grows (lower arcseconds = sharper)" widget="plotter" config={rayleighCfg} />

  <h2>The electromagnetic spectrum: the universe's many channels</h2>
  <p>
    Visible light spans wavelengths from about 400 nm (violet) to 700 nm (red). But hot gas,
    cold dust, spinning galaxies and black holes all emit across the whole spectrum, and each
    band carries different news:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Radio (metre to millimetre):</strong> penetrates dust, reveals pulsars, quasars and the cosmic microwave background — the universe's cold, faint and ancient channels. Radio telescopes can also work in daylight and bad weather.</li>
    <li><strong>Infrared:</strong> sees through dust clouds where stars are born; essential for distant (redshifted) galaxies. James Webb's 6.5-metre gold-coated mirror is an infrared machine, and it must be cooled to about −230 °C so its own warmth doesn't blind it.</li>
    <li><strong>Optical:</strong> the human-familiar band — where large mirrors and adaptive optics (laser-corrected "artificial guide stars") let ground telescopes approach space sharpness.</li>
    <li><strong>Ultraviolet:</strong> hot young stars and the atmospheres of planets — absorbed by ozone, so it needs orbit.</li>
    <li><strong>X-ray and gamma:</strong> the violent universe — black-hole accretion, supernova remnants. These photons stop in matter, so the telescopes orbit above the atmosphere (Chandra, Fermi), and X-ray mirrors must reflect at grazing angles like stones skimming water.</li>
    <li><strong>Gravitational waves</strong> (detected from 2015) are the newest channel entirely: spacetime itself as the detector. Multi-messenger astronomy — seeing one event in light and in gravity — is the field's newest superpower.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Why don't we just build one telescope for everything? Because the <strong>atmosphere is a
    filter</strong>, and only two windows reach the ground cleanly: visible light and radio.
    Ozone eats ultraviolet, water vapour swallows infrared and microwaves, and the dense air
    below absorbs X-rays and gamma rays outright. That filter is protective for life (it is the
    reason you are not irradiated) and obstructive for astronomy — it is the single reason orbit
    observatories exist. Each orbiting telescope is a mission to a wavelength the ground can
    never see. The history of the off-window bands is a lesson in instrument-driven discovery.
    Radio astronomy began in 1932 when Karl Jansky, an engineer hunting radio static for Bell
    Labs, noticed the interference peaked exactly 4 minutes earlier each day — the signature of
    the stars, not the Sun. Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson found the cosmic microwave background
    in 1964 while debugging a horn antenna's unexplainable hiss (after excluding pigeon droppings
    as the cause). X-ray astronomy started with a Geiger counter on a captured V-2 rocket in
    1949. In every case the channel existed; the detector came first, and the universe followed.
  </p>

  <h2>Galileo's discoveries: the telescope as evidence</h2>
  <p>
    In 1609 Galileo turned a 20-power spyglass skyward — not the first to try, but the first to
    publish what it proved. Over eighteen months he logged four results that broke the
    geocentric world. <strong>The Moon had mountains and craters</strong>: its edge was rough,
    not the perfect sphere philosophy demanded — his measurements of the terminator's shadows let
    him estimate mountain heights, some taller than any then known on Earth.
    <strong>Jupiter had four moons of its own</strong>
    (Io, Europa, Ganymede, Callisto), circling Jupiter — so not everything orbited the Earth.
    <strong>Venus showed phases</strong> like the Moon's, from crescent to gibbous: under the
    Ptolemaic model Venus could only ever show crescents, so the full-Venus phases proved Venus
    circled the Sun. And <strong>the Sun had spots</strong> that moved with its rotation — the
    "perfect" Sun was blemished and turning. Each observation is a modest-looking thing through
    a poor lens; together they ended two thousand years of cosmology. The lesson generalises:
    a new instrument is not a better view of the same universe — it is a different universe.
    The same pattern repeated with every later telescope class: radio found pulsars (Jocelyn
    Bell, 1967), X-rays found accreting black holes, infrared found the star-forming nurseries
    hidden behind dust. What the eye cannot see, it cannot even suspect.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "telescopes mostly magnify." For today's large telescopes,
    magnification is nearly irrelevant — the deepest imaging surveys run at modest zoom.
    What aperture buys is <em>light</em> (detecting a galaxy whose photons arrive a billion per
    square metre per second — one photon per minute on a small patch of your retina) and
    <em>resolution</em> (separating what is actually two stars, or mapping a galaxy's
    structure). Magnification is eyepiece arithmetic; aperture is capability.</p>
  <p>
    It is also worth being precise about what an image is. A telescope does not "show" a
    distant galaxy the way a window shows a mountain: it accumulates photons over minutes or
    hours onto a detector, each pixel counting arrivals. Everything about the result — the
    faintness reachable, the detail resolved, the colour information — is set by how many
    photons the objective intercepts and how faithfully they are counted. That is why
    professional "observing" is closer to running a counting experiment than to looking:
    exposure time is a real cost, telescope time is scheduled in committee-reviewed blocks, and
    the difference between a 4-metre and an 8-metre telescope is not what you can see but what
    you can measure in the same night. The eye is still the mind's instrument; the telescope
    merely feeds it photons the eye could never catch.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Ground-based optical astronomy fights one more enemy: turbulence. Air of different
    temperatures at different heights jitters the image — "twinkling" from the ground, a smeared
    blur through a big telescope, limited in practice to about 1 arcsecond no matter the
    aperture. <strong>Adaptive optics</strong> beats it by measuring the distortion a thousand
    times a second and bending a small deformable mirror to cancel it, using a bright reference
    — a natural guide star or a laser excites sodium atoms at 90 km altitude to make an
    artificial one. With AO, an 8-metre telescope regains its true diffraction limit of about
    0.03 arcseconds, out-resolving Hubble; space telescopes remain superior for infrared and
    stability, but the ground has fought back. Space itself has costs to manage too: Hubble's
    primary mirror was famously ground 2 micrometres too flat — a hair-thin error that blurred
    every early image until a 1993 servicing flight installed corrective optics, essentially
    eyeglasses for a telescope. Even in orbit, the physics of diffraction and the human craft
    of engineering remain inseparable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Resolution can also be bought with distance rather than diameter. Two radio dishes
    thousands of kilometres apart, combined as <strong>interferometers</strong>, resolve as if
    they were one dish of that separation — the basis of very-long-baseline interferometry,
    which maps quasars to milliarcsecond precision. The Event Horizon Telescope pushed it to
    the limit: eight observatories on four continents, correlating petabytes of data, resolved
    the shadow of M87's central black hole in 2019 with an effective aperture the diameter of
    Earth — an angular resolution of about 20 microarcseconds, comparable to reading a
    newspaper in Paris from New York. Radio astronomy also owns the spectrum's history: 21 cm
    hydrogen emission — the spin-flip line — maps the gas between stars (the Milky Way lesson
    uses it), and the cosmic microwave background is a radio observation that rewrote cosmology.
    The next decade belongs to wide eyes: the Vera Rubin Observatory will photograph the entire
    southern sky every few nights, and the Square Kilometre Array will gather radio data at
    rates measured in exabytes per year. The instrument is still the science — Galileo's rule,
    four centuries on.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NASA mission pages (Hubble, JWST, Chandra); LIGO Scientific Collaboration; OpenStax, Astronomy 2e (Telescopes); Pearson Edexcel GCSE Astronomy specification, topic 11.</p>
